From bfb525a399600386834bb792b380894bd16c55eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Roscoe <bryanroscoe@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:27:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 18/30] Jace, Multiverse Architect: creatures can't attack
 Jaces you control

Jace, Multiverse Architect: "At the beginning of combat on each
opponent's turn, they may pay {2}. If they don't, creatures they control
can't attack Jaces you control this turn."

phase left "creatures they control can't attack Jaces you control"
unimplemented, so an opponent who didn't pay attacked Jace anyway, and
the trigger also fired on its controller's own turn.

- CR 603.1: "at the beginning of combat on each opponent's turn" now
  carries the opponents'-turn constraint (oracle_trigger.rs; the
  "on <turn>" suffix reads the same table as "during <turn>"). Champions
  of Minas Tirith shares the wording.
- CR 205.3j + CR 508.1c: new AttackTargetFilter::PlaneswalkerOfType
  ("Jaces you control"): planeswalkers of that type the restriction's
  controller controls, parsed by the defended-scope grammar.
- CR 611.2c: a "can't attack" restriction from a resolving ability
  changes no characteristics, so it also affects creatures that come
  under that player's control later. The effect parser now emits the
  attack-restriction grant for a broadcast creature population, and
  register_transient_effect installs it as one transient effect with the
  population filter intact (like the MustAttackAwayFromSource branch),
  fixing a player the filter names through the resolving ability ("they"
  of a phase trigger, a trigger's player, a target) as SpecificPlayer.
  Chronomantic Escape's "until your next turn, creatures can't attack
  you" takes the same path.

The {2} payment itself is fixed in the next commit (the payer was the
ability's controller, not "they").

Card data: the parse change reaches games only with card data generated
by this parser.

Test: tests/integration/jace_multiverse_architect_attack_restriction.rs.
The parser test that pinned Chronomantic Escape and Web of Inertia as
gaps now expects them claimed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
